# ProviderBridge Side-by-Side (Fixture Lane)

```elixir
Mix.install([{:nx_quantum, path: "../.."}])
```

This notebook runs the same lifecycle workload (submit, poll, fetch result) against `IBM Runtime`, `AWS Braket` and `Azure Quantum`, and checks that the three return the same normalized result shape.

Scope and limits:

* It runs the fixture lane only. No network call is made and no provider account is needed.
* The tokens, profiles and workspace names below are fake. They exist only to satisfy the typed configuration check.
* The live environment keys are shown as text in section 8 and are never read or set. A test (`test/nx_quantum/livebooks_test.exs`) fails if a notebook cell reads one.
* The fixture lane shows that the lifecycle contract is typed and uniform. It says nothing about the outcomes of a live QPU run.

`NxQuantum.ProviderBridge.run_lifecycle/3`, `submit_job/3` and the provider adapter modules are on the experimental tier in `docs/api-stability.md`.

## 1) Aliases and shared payload

```elixir
alias NxQuantum.Adapters.Providers.AwsBraket
alias NxQuantum.Adapters.Providers.AzureQuantum
alias NxQuantum.Adapters.Providers.IBMRuntime
alias NxQuantum.ProviderBridge
alias NxQuantum.ProviderBridge.Serialization

payload = %{workflow: :sampler, shots: 1024}
```

## 2) Provider option packs

```elixir
provider_cases = [
  {:ibm_runtime, IBMRuntime,
   [
     target: "ibm_backend_simulator",
     provider_config: %{auth_token: "fake-ibm-token", channel: "ibm_cloud", backend: "ibm_backend_simulator"}
   ]},
  {:aws_braket, AwsBraket,
   [
     target: "arn:aws:braket:::device/quantum-simulator/amazon/sv1",
     provider_config: %{
       region: "us-east-1",
       credentials_profile: "default",
       device_arn: "arn:aws:braket:::device/quantum-simulator/amazon/sv1"
     }
   ]},
  {:azure_quantum, AzureQuantum,
   [
     target: "azure.quantum.sim",
     provider_config: %{
       workspace: "ws-1",
       auth_context: "managed_identity",
       target_id: "azure.quantum.sim",
       provider_name: "microsoft"
     }
   ]}
]
```

## 3) Execute side-by-side lifecycle

```elixir
results =
  Enum.map(provider_cases, fn {provider_id, adapter, opts} ->
    {provider_id, ProviderBridge.run_lifecycle(adapter, payload, opts)}
  end)

results
```

## 4) Compare normalized result keys

Each `result` is a `NxQuantum.ProviderBridge.Result` struct. The comparison uses its field names, without the `:__struct__` marker.

```elixir
# checkpoint: normalized_key_sets
normalized_key_sets =
  Enum.map(results, fn {provider_id, {:ok, %{result: result}}} ->
    {provider_id, result |> Map.from_struct() |> Map.keys() |> Enum.sort()}
  end)

normalized_key_sets
```

The common fields are `:job_id`, `:state`, `:provider`, `:target`, `:payload`, `:metadata`, `:schema_version`, `:request_id`, `:correlation_id` and `:idempotency_key`.

```elixir
# checkpoint: deterministic_shape
[{_first_id, first_keys} | rest] = normalized_key_sets

%{
  same_keys_for_all_providers: Enum.all?(rest, fn {_provider_id, keys} -> keys == first_keys end),
  states: Enum.map(results, fn {id, {:ok, %{result: result}}} -> {id, result.state} end)
}
```

Equal key sets mean the three providers return the same normalized result structure. It does not mean their payloads agree.

## 5) Fixture-lane job ids

In the fixture lane the job id is derived from the payload and the provider, so it is the same on every run, and the job completes. The cell records both for each provider.

```elixir
# checkpoint: fixture_job_ids
Enum.map(results, fn {provider_id, {:ok, %{result: result}}} ->
  {provider_id, %{job_id: result.job_id, state: result.state, provider: result.provider}}
end)
```

## 6) Serialize and rehydrate

`Serialization.to_external_map/1` turns a result into a versioned (`"v1"`) map with string keys. `Serialization.from_external_map/1` reads it back into a typed struct. `docs/api-stability.md` ("Envelope Compatibility Policy") documents both and the round-trip rule: encoding the rehydrated value gives back the same map.

```elixir
# checkpoint: envelope_round_trip
Enum.map(results, fn {provider_id, {:ok, %{result: result}}} ->
  external = Serialization.to_external_map(result)
  {:ok, rehydrated} = Serialization.from_external_map(external)

  {provider_id,
   %{
     type: external["type"],
     schema_version: external["schema_version"],
     state: external["state"],
     provider: external["provider"],
     round_trip_equal: Serialization.to_external_map(rehydrated) == external
   }}
end)
```

Payloads that contain structs, such as an `Nx.Tensor`, are not serializable today (KG-14 in `docs/roadmap.md`). The fixture payloads here are plain maps.

## 7) A typed error: missing configuration

Without the required provider configuration the call returns `{:error, %NxQuantum.ProviderBridge.ProviderError{}}`. It does not raise, and the error names the missing keys.

```elixir
# checkpoint: missing_config_errors
Enum.map(provider_cases, fn {provider_id, adapter, opts} ->
  {:error, error} = ProviderBridge.submit_job(adapter, payload, Keyword.put(opts, :provider_config, %{}))

  {provider_id, %{code: error.code, reason: error.reason, missing_keys: error.metadata.missing_keys}}
end)
```

## 8) Live environment keys (text only)

Live transport needs all three of these:

1. The request asks for it: `transport_mode: :live` or `transport_mode: :live_smoke` in the options.
2. The matching environment key is enabled, either the global key or the provider key. The value must be `1` or `true`.
3. Every required provider config key is present. A request with a missing config key stays in the fixture lane.

If any of the three is missing, a `:live_smoke` request falls back to the fixture lane. A `:live` request returns a typed `{:error, _}` instead. This notebook asks for neither, so it never leaves the fixture lane.

The keys, as text. This notebook never sets or reads them:

```text
NXQ_PROVIDER_LIVE                          global gate for live transport
NXQ_PROVIDER_LIVE_IBM_RUNTIME              provider gate for live transport
NXQ_PROVIDER_LIVE_AWS_BRAKET               provider gate for live transport
NXQ_PROVIDER_LIVE_AZURE_QUANTUM            provider gate for live transport
NXQ_PROVIDER_LIVE_SMOKE                    global gate for live-smoke checks
NXQ_PROVIDER_LIVE_SMOKE_<PROVIDER>         provider gate for live-smoke checks
```

The optional live-smoke gates are described in `docs/release-process.md`. Live lanes need real credentials. The claim for them is a typed lifecycle, not a deterministic outcome: a live QPU result depends on the device and its queue.

## Checkpoints

Cells that start with `# checkpoint: name` are compared against recorded values by `test/nx_quantum/livebooks_test.exs`. Run `mix test.livebooks` from the repository root to check this notebook.
